  "query_preview": "/TUESDAY NIGHT THROUGH SUNDAY/...\nIssued at 302 AM CST Mon Nov 2 2026\n\nRain largely comes to an end from west to east overnight Tuesday \ninto Wednesday morning, with the (likely occluded by this point) \nfront lagging an hour or two behind the clearing line. Some \nanafrontal showers can be expected on Wednesday across the region, \nthough there will be plenty of dry time. Winds behind the front will \nbe more westerly, if not southwesterly, which won't allow for a \ndrastic airmass change and significant cooling. Still, dewpoints \nwill fall into the 40s and temperatures will drop compared to \nprevious days and approach more usual values for early November. \nSituations like these (dry air rushing in without significant \ncooling) can lend themselves to elevated fire danger, however the \nwidespread wetting rain on Tuesday will tamp down any fire threat.\n\nAloft, amidst longwave troughing that encompasses most of the CONUS, \nthe closed wave affecting our weather will gradually shunt \ndownstream as additional reinforcing waves arrive. While largely \nmoisture-starved due to the longwave stunting any Gulf influence, \nthey should feature enough forcing to result in some light \nprecipitation at times (particularly on Friday, when another cold \nfront arrives). Exactly where and when these occur is unsure, but \nLREF ensemble guidance suggests areas of light precipitation \n(less than 0.05 inches) intermittently during this time. \nTemperatures will be held in check as a combination of weak cold\nadvection and cloud cover stunt any diurnal warming. Additional\nwaves track through the region this weekend into early next \nweek, bringing more precipitation chances, but confidence in \nthis aspect of the forecast is quite low. It does seem likely \nthat, while somewhat more normal temperatures remain favored \ninto the end of the week, no signs of a dramatic cooldown are \nevident beyond that.",
